\section*{Supplemental material}

To study the dependence of our results on the nucleon momenta, which are uncertain, in Fig.~\ref{fig:oxygentransmutationhalf}, we repeat the computation shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:oxygentransmutation}, but with the initial nucleon momenta in each nucleus ($p_\text{fermi}$) halved, as well as the maximum distance to combine into a cluster (\deltap{})\@.
Doing this narrows the momentum distribution of each species, and as such the circulating isotope production cross-section is larger by about a factor 2 for the most prevalently produced isotopes.

Secondly, we repeat our analysis of the circulating isotope production for $^{20}$Ne$^{20}$Ne and $p^{16}$O collisions, the results of which can be seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:neontransmutation} and Fig.~\ref{fig:poxygentransmutation}, respectively.
For $^{20}$Ne$^{20}$Ne, the production of $^4$He is enhanced by 14\% with respect to what one would expect from a naive scaling with the cross-section.
This can likely be explained by the bowling pin shape of $^{20}$Ne \cite{Giacalone:2024luz}, which perhaps makes it more likely that the $\alpha$-cluster forming the `tip' of the bowling pin misses the collision to become spectators.

%We next study a few basic observables for collisions of contaminants with oxygen.\gn{Note: in Figs. 3, 4 and 5 the collision species (except $p$ and $d$) should technically be upright. Also, in Fig. 4 the centrality should also be upright.} Firstly, in Fig.~\ref{fig:ntrackdistributions}, we show the distribution of charged particles per event $N_\text{ch}$\@. One can see that the most commonly produced contaminant, $^4$He, upon colliding with $^{16}$O produces much fewer particles, meaning that above around $N_\text{ch} = 150$, $^{16}$O$^{16}$O collisions are essentially uncontaminated. However, more peripheral $^{16}$O$^{16}$O collisions would be similar in number of particles produced to contaminants like $^4$He$^{16}$O\@.

