\title{Elliptic Curves over Finite Fields and the Hopf Fibration}


\author{Nadir Hajouji\textsuperscript{1} and Steve Trettel\textsuperscript{2}
\vspace{10pt}\\
\textsuperscript{1}nhajouji@gmail.com\\
\textsuperscript{2}University of San Francisco; strettel@usfca.edu} % 
\date{}					% Suppress any date on submissions

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{document}


\maketitle

% Prevent page number 1 from being printed on the first page.
\thispagestyle{empty}

\vspace{-0.7cm}

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a new method for visualizing elliptic curves over finite fields that preserves their full algebraic structure, while also producing beautiful and accurate illustrations in $\RR^3$.  
\end{abstract}

\vspace{0.2cm}

\begin{minipage}[b]{0.2\textwidth} 
\hspace{-0.5cm} \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/EllipticOriginal.png}
\end{minipage}
\begin{minipage}[b]{0.75\textwidth} 

If you ask an algebraically inclined person to define an elliptic curve,
they will probably say something like 
\emph{"an elliptic curve is a genus one curve with a choice of marked point"}.  If you then look for a visualization, 
you'll often come across an implicit curve 
containing the solutions $(x,y)$ to a plane cubic
such as $y^2=x^3-x$ to the left.
Such pictures show only a poor shadow of the truth in characteristic 0, 
where the marked point (essential to defining the group structure) 
is thrown off to infinity, 
and the donut-like shape one expects of a genus-1 object 
is visible only in two \emph{complex} dimensions, 
and even there only with some difficulty\footnotemark.

 But these \emph{plot-the-solutions} type images fare even worse in characteristic $p$ where the ambient space $\FF_{p^n}\times\FF_{p^n}$ is at once both a finite point set and a high dimensional vector space over $\FF_p$: see Figure 4 for an attempt that leaves much to be desired.
\end{minipage}
\footnotetext{Even four dimensional readers will find the solution set in $\CC^2$ does not look like the donuts one knows and loves. Rather, with the marked point still thrown off to infinity, its a surface of infinite area whose tasty nature is only realized after \emph{compactification}. }

 \vspace{-0.3cm}

In this paper we propose a new means of visualizing elliptic curves mod $p$, replacing the ambient space $\FF_{p^n}\times\FF_{p^n}$ with a characteristic zero elliptic curve, 
which we then embed conformally in $\RR^3$ as a Riemann surface.  
It turns out that one can do this in a way that preserves all of the key algebraic structure of the elliptic curve mod $p$, but leads to significantly better illustrations.  



\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.93\textwidth]{img/FrontCover.jpg}
	\caption{Our new illustrations: the 640 points of $y^2=x^3+3x \pmod{5}$ over $\FF_{625}$ (orange), and the 2379 points of $y^2=x^3+3 \pmod{7}$ over $\FF_{2401}$ (blue). The marked point on each is highlighted.}
	\label{fig:bestPic}
\end{figure}


\newpage


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{Lifts and Visualizations}


To \emph{lift} a mod $p$ elliptic curve (or more generally a commutative algebraic group) $X$ to the characteristic 0 world, we seek a corresponding curve/group $\tilde{X}$ defined over the algebraic integers which somehow '\emph{remembers the original characteristic $p$ algebra}'.\footnote{In algebraic language, this means we want to lift the Galois module structure of the characteristic 0 object}.   That is, we want $\tilde{X}$ to know about both (1) the intrinsic group structure and of $X$ (2) the action of the Galois group $G=\mathrm{Gal}(\FF_p^{\mathrm{sep}}/\FF_p)$ on $X$.
The Galois group $G$ is procyclic, generated by the Frobenius $x\mapsto x^p$;
so the action of $G$ on a commutative algebraic group is completely determined by a single map, namely the endomorphism $\phi : X \to X$ induced by Frobenius.\footnote{If $X$ is a variety with coordinates $(x_1,..x_n)$ then the induced map is $(x_1,..x_n)\mapsto (x_1^p,..,x_n^p)$.}
Accordingly, we define a \emph{characteristic zero lift} to be a commutative algebraic group $\tilde{X}$,
together with an endomorphism $\tilde{\phi}: \tilde{X}\to \tilde{X}$,
such that $\tilde{X}, \tilde{\phi}$ reduce to $X, \phi$ mod $p$ respectively.

If one is lucky enough to be in possession of a lift $(\tilde{X},\tilde{\phi})$ of their favorite elliptic curve $X$, 
then we can, indeed, see the full Galois module structure in characteristic 0:
the fixed points of $\tilde{\phi}$ in $\tilde{X}$ form a subgroup
that, upon reduction modulo \(p\), is isomorphic to the  group of $\FF_p$-points of $X$, and more generally, 
the subgroup of $\tilde{X}$ fixed by $\tilde{\phi}^n$ corresponds isomorphically to the $\FF_{p^n}$-points of $X$. Consequently, provided we can visualize $\tilde{X}$, a lift provides uniform representations of the $\FF_{p^n}$-points for any $n$, faithfully capturing both the group structure and the Galois action through the morphisms of $\tilde{X}$.



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{Baby's First Lift: The Multiplicative Group}

Before tackling elliptic curves, we illustrate the main ideas with a simple example: the multiplicative group\footnote{This is the group scheme whose $K$-points are isomorphic to the group of nonzero elements of $K$ under multiplication.} of a finite field. 
The standard way of visualizing this space is to identify $\FF_{p^n}$
with an $n$-dimensional vector space over $\FF_p$
(analogous to viewing $\CC$ as $\RR^2$) and plot its nonzero points, as in Figure \ref{fig:mult-vs}. 
However, this approach suffers from several drawbacks, 
including (1)  the vector space structure does not naturally reflect the intrinsic group law or the Galois action, obscuring the very algebra we are trying to visualize, and 
(2) it is impractical for most $n$ as higher-dimensional vector spaces cannot be easily visualized in $\RR^3$.

\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/Mult_VS.jpg}
	\caption{The multiplicative group of $\FF_9$ (left) and $\FF_{27}$ (right).  In blue is the Cayley graph for the group structure ($\ZZ/8\ZZ$ and $\ZZ/{26}\ZZ$ respectively), and green depicts the action of the Frobenius map.}
	\label{fig:mult-vs}
\end{figure}

Everything improves dramatically if we instead compute a characteristic zero lift.  A natural choice for $\tilde{X}$ is the multiplicative group of nonzero complex numbers, onto which the mod $p$ Frobenius map lifts to $\tilde{\phi}\colon z\mapsto z^p$.  To recover the $\FF_{p^n}$ valued points from this lift, we compute the fixed points of $\tilde{\phi}^n$, which are the $(p^{n}-1)^{th}$ roots of unity\footnote{$\tilde{\phi}^n(z)=(z^{p})^n=z$ implies $z^{p^n-1}=1$}.  This gives a single 2-dimensional picture which can be used for all $n$, and faithfully preserves the intrinsic geometry: group multiplication is realized by \emph{rotation} of the unit circle, and the Galois action is the restriction of the circle's $p$-fold covering map to the lifted subgroup.



% Our proposal instead lifts the $\FF_{p^n}$ points to a subset of a characteristic zero variety, and identifies them with the appropriate roots of unity in $\CC$.  To see this, note the Frobenius map $\phi : X \to X$ looks like $t\mapsto t^p$.
% Now, let $\tilde{X} = \GG_m$ (over $\CC$) and let $\widetilde{\phi}$ be the map $t\mapsto t^p$.
% The pair $(\tilde{X},\tilde{\phi})$ is a lift of $X$,
% and the $\FF_{p^n}$-valued points of $X$ are the nonzero complex numbers that are fixed by $x\mapsto x^{p^n}$.
% \footnote{This is equivalent to saying that $x^{p^n-1} = 1$,
% so the $\FF_{p^n}$-valued points are precisely the $(p^n-1)$th roots of unity.}


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/Mult_Lift.jpg}
	\caption{Lifting multiplicative groups of $\FF_9$ (left) and $\FF_{27}$ (right) to characteristic zero identifies them with the appropriate roots of unity.  Blue depict the group structure and green the action of Frobenius.}
	\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}





%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{Lifting Elliptic Curves}

We now explain how to do with elliptic curves mod $p$ what we did with the multiplicative group.
To simplify the exposition, we're going to focus on a specific elliptic curve.

\vspace{-0.25cm}

\[ E: \quad y^2 = x^3 + 3x \pmod 5\]

One might wish to visualize these objects by \emph{plotting solution pairs} $(x,y)\in\FF_{p^n}\times\FF_{p^n}$ as in Figure \ref{fig:elliptic-vs}.  But this suffers from familiar problems: one point is out at infinity, the intrinsic (group) and extrinsic (vector space) operations are mismatched, and an explosion of dimension\footnote{Again, perhaps our 4-dimensional readers can better assemble a view of $\FF_{25}\times\FF_{25}$ from the slices of a hypercube presented here.}
renders all but the simplest case unwieldy.


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/VS_Embedding.jpg}
	\caption{The elliptic curve $y^2=x^3+3x \pmod{5}$, over $\FF_5$ (left) and $\FF_{25}$ (right), shown with the Cayley graphs of their group structures ($\ZZ/10\ZZ$ and $\ZZ/2\ZZ\times\ZZ/10\ZZ$ respectively).}
	\label{fig:elliptic-vs}
\end{figure}

Having learned our lesson, we instead try to lift this curve, and seek a pair $(\widetilde{E}, \widetilde{\phi})$ that reduce mod 5 to $E$ and the Frobenius map $\phi(x,y)=(x^5,y^5)$.  The curve is easy: we define $\widetilde{E}: y^2 = x^3 + 3x$ by the same equation, interpreted in characteristic zero. This curve famously has an ``extra" automorphism given by $(x,y)\mapsto (-x,iy)$, which we used to compute an explicit formula for the lift of Frobenius map:

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
    \tilde{\phi_1}\\
    \tilde{\phi_2}
\end{pmatrix}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{x \left(3 \left(x^8-12 x^6-138 x^4-108 x^2+81\right)+4 i \left(x^8+18 x^6-162 x^2-81\right)\right)}{\left(5 x^4+6 x^2+9\right)^2}\\
    \tfrac{-x(x^2+3)(2 \left(x^{12}+78 x^{10}+999 x^8-1404 x^6-6561 x^4-1458 x^2+729\right) + i \left(11 x^{12}-42 x^{10}+1809 x^8+8964 x^6+1053 x^4-7290 x^2-729\right))}{\left(5 x^4+6 x^2+9\right)^3 y}
\end{pmatrix}$$

Additional calculation\footnote{
% Now we argue that $\tilde{\phi}$ ``reduces to Frobenius mod 5."
Let $\mathfrak{p} \subset \ZZ[i]$ be the ideal generated by $-2+i$.
Then $\ZZ[i]/\mathfrak{pr}$ is isomorphic to $\ZZ/5\ZZ$,
so we can use the quotient-by-$\mathfrak{p}$ morphism to reduce Gaussian integers mod 5.
For our purposes, ``reducing $\tilde{\phi}$ mod 5" means we will replace $i$ by 2 in the formula for $\widetilde{\phi}$, so that the coefficients in our formula for $\widetilde{\phi}$ are all integers, and then reduce those integers mod 5 to obtain a rational function over $\FF_5$.
Doing so shows that: $\widetilde{\phi_1}(x,y) \equiv \frac{x^9+3 x^7+x^5}{x^4+3 x^2+1} = \frac{x^5 (1 + x)^2 (4 + x)^2}{(1 + x)^2 (4 + x)^2} = x^5\pmod 5$, and similarly for $\tilde{\phi}_2$.
}
confirms that this proposed map does indeed reduce to $(x,y)\mapsto (x^5,y^5)$ mod 5, so $(\tilde{E},\tilde{\phi})$ really is a lift of $E$.  
Theoretically we are done, but practically this leaves two problems: (1) the coordinates we chose for $\tilde{E}$ realize it in four dimensions and (2) the formula for $\phi$ is ridiculous to work with. 
These are simultaneously solved by \emph{uniformization}, which in our example gives an isomorphism of the complex variety $\tilde{E}$, as a Riemann surface, with the flat torus $\CC/\Lambda$ for $\Lambda = \ZZ \oplus i\ZZ $.
Furthermore, in these coordinates the lift $\widetilde{\phi}$ is given by the remarkably simple formula:
    \[\tilde{\phi}\colon\quad z\pmod \Lambda \mapsto (-2+i)z\pmod \Lambda \]


Uniformizing allows visualization of $\tilde{E}$ by choosing a fundamental domain for $\CC/\Lambda$.  Incredibly, the simple form of $\tilde{\phi}$ allows an entirely geometric description of the mod $p$ points: $\tilde{\phi}$ fixes $\frac{1}{-3+i} \Lambda \pmod \Lambda$, so the points over $\FF_{p^n}$ form a scaled down copies of the original lattice with addition modulo side pairings.
To avoid the discontinuities inherent in such a choice of fundamental domain, we further embed $\CC/\Lambda$ as a torus in $\RR^3$ using a conformal map—which preserves its structure as a Riemann surface (Figure \ref{fig:elliptic-lift-embed}). 
 For example, one may achieve this by adjusting the isometry $s+it\mapsto (e^{is},e^{it})\in \CC^2$
 with suitable homotheties and following with stereographic projection to $\RR^3$.

\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/4_Lattice.jpg}
	\caption{The points of  $y^2=x^3+3x\pmod 5$  over $\FF_5$ (left pair) and $\FF_{25}$ (right pair), embedded in the Riemann surface $\CC/(\ZZ\oplus i\ZZ)$, viewed as a fundamental domain.  Images show the group elements as a scaled lattice, as well the Cayley graph for a choice of generating set: compare Figure \ref{fig:elliptic-vs}.}
	\label{fig:elliptic-lift-fd}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/4_Torus.jpg}
	\caption{The points of  $y^2=x^3+3x\pmod 5$ over $\FF_5$ (left) and $\FF_{25}$ (center, right), lifted to the Riemann surface $\CC/(\ZZ\oplus i\ZZ)$ and then conformally embedded in $\RR^3$. The final image removes a portion of the torus surface to better show the Cayley graph of the elliptic curve: compare with Figure \ref{fig:elliptic-vs}. }
	\label{fig:elliptic-lift-embed}
\end{figure}



\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/4_Gallery.jpg}
	\caption{The fixed points of higher powers of $\tilde{\phi}$ yields the points of $y^2=x^3+3x\pmod 5$ over all extensions of $\FF_5$.  Here are the points defined over $\FF_{125}$ (left), $\FF_{625}$ (center) and $\FF_{3125}$ (right).}
	\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}




%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{A Gallery of Elliptic Curves Mod p}

We used $y^2 \equiv x^3 + 3x \pmod 5$ to illustrate the lifting process as the equations involved are essentially as simple as possible\footnote{We could have chosen an example where the formula for $\widetilde{\phi}$ is simpler by working in characteristic 2 or 3 instead of 5, but any elliptic curvologist would tell you to avoid those characteristics unless you have special training.}, but it can all be done with any elliptic curve over any field of prime order:

\begin{itemize}
    \item By \emph{Deuring lifting},  we can lift any $E/\FF_p$ to a pair $(\tilde{E},\tilde{\phi})$ defined over the algebraic integers.
    \item The theory of \emph{elliptic curves with complex multiplication} guarantees that we can find a (unique up to isomorphism) Riemann surface of the form $(\CC/\Lambda, z\pmod\Lambda\mapsto \alpha z \pmod \Lambda)$ with $\alpha\in\Lambda$
    % \footnote{Here $\alpha$ is a complex number - in the example, we had $\alpha = -2+i$.} 
    that represents $(\widetilde{E},\widetilde{\phi})$. See \cite{coxprimes} for an excellent introduction to the algebraic/arithmetic aspects of this story.

    \item Work of Garsia \cite{garsia} guarantees the existence of conformal embeddings of Riemann surfaces into $\RR^3$. For tori $\CC/\Lambda$, further work of Pinkall \cite{pinkall} gives an explicit construction (reviewed in the following section).

\end{itemize}

Following this procedure for the elliptic curve $E\colon \,y^2=x^3+3 \pmod 7$ identifies a characteristic zero lift whose analytic model is the quotient of $\CC$ by the hexagonal lattice $\ZZ\oplus\omega\ZZ$ for $\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}$. The lift of Frobenius acts as multiplication by $\alpha=-2+\omega$ modulo the lattice, whose $n^{th}$ power fixes a copy of the lattice rescaled by $1/(\alpha^n-1)$.
We can use this immediately to visualize the $\FF_{7^n}$ points of $E$ in a fundamental domain, and after tracking down a conformal embedding to $\RR^3$, produce the beautiful images in Figures \ref{fig:hex-1},\ref{fig:hex-2}.


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/3_Intro.jpg}
	\caption{(Left) To embed $E:y^2=x^3+3\pmod 7$ in $\RR^3$, we need a conformal embedding of the hexagonal torus $\CC/\Lambda$: there are many possibilities, such as these two. (Right) the $\FF_{49}$ points of $E$ form a cyclic group $\cong \ZZ/39\ZZ$, which we may visualize on the fundamental domain or torus surface. }
	\label{fig:hex-1}
\end{figure}


We resist the common mathematical temptation to immediately ask '\emph{what's next}' and pause to ponder and enjoy the lifts of several elliptic curves below (Figures \ref{fig:D7}, \ref{fig:D8}, \ref{fig:D11}).  Additionally, we note this lifting process applies not just mod $p$, but also to give new images of the \emph{real points} (Figure \ref{fig:RealPts}).


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/3_Gallery.jpg}
	\caption{Points of $E\colon \,y^2=x^3+3\pmod 7$ over the fields $\FF_{343}$ (left), $\FF_{2401}$ (center) and $\FF_{16,807}$ (right).  The intrinsic hexagonal symmetries of $E$ 
 - otherwise rather hidden - are clearly apparent in the lift. }
	\label{fig:hex-2}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/7_Gallery.jpg}
	\caption{The elliptic curve $y^2=x^3+5x+7\pmod {11}$ over $\FF_{121}$ (left), $\FF_{1331}$ (middle) and $\FF_{14,641}$ (right).}
	\label{fig:D7}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/-8_Gallery.jpg}
	\caption{The elliptic curve $y^2=x^3+x+3\pmod {11}$ over $\FF_{121}$ (left), $\FF_{1331}$ (middle) and $\FF_{14,641}$ (right).}
	\label{fig:D8}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/11_Gallery.jpg}
	\caption{The elliptic curve $y^2=x^3+x+1 \pmod 5$  over $\FF_{125}$ (left), $\FF_{625}$ (middle) and $\FF_{3125}$ (right).}
	\label{fig:D11}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{img/RealLifts.jpg}
	\caption{The real elliptic curves $y^2=x^3+3x$ (red), $y^2=x^3+1$ (green) and $y^2=x^3-x$ (orange) in the plane (left) and lifted to the uniformized complex curve (right). Marked point is black on each.}
	\label{fig:RealPts}
\end{figure}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{Characteristic Zero Curves \& The Hopf Fibration}


We've already used an incredible amount of beautiful results from algebra to lift and uniformize mod $p$ curves, and we close the paper with a brief discussion of the beautiful topology that goes into realizing the uniformizations in $\RR^3$.  dThe star of the show is the Hopf fibration, a topologically nontrivial map $\eta\colon\SS^3\to\SS^2$ given by $\eta(z,w)= z/w$ for unit vectors $(z,w)\in\CC^2$ and $z/w\in \CC\cup\infty$ identified with the Riemann sphere.

\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{img/hopf.jpeg}
	\caption{The Hopf fibration $\eta\colon\SS^3\to\SS^2$ viewed under stereographic projection.  Point preimages are cirlces, any two of which are linked (left); the peimage of a curve is an annulus (right).}
	\label{fig:hopf}
\end{figure}

In \cite{pinkall}, Pinkall uses the Hopf map to prove every genus one Riemann surface embeds in $\RR^3$, by showing they embed in $\SS^3$ and applying stereographic projection.  Specifically he shows (1) that the preimage of any closed curve on $\SS^2$ is a flat torus in $\SS^3$, (2) all flat metrics arise in this way.  Crucially for us, the arugment explicitly identifies the moduli:
the preimage $\eta^{-1}(C)$ of a simple closed curve $C$ on $\mathbb{S}^2$ of length $L$ enclosing area $A < 2\pi$ is isometric to $\mathbb{C} / \Lambda$, for $\Lambda = 2\pi \ZZ\oplus (\tfrac{A}{2}+i\tfrac{L}{2}) \ZZ$.  
Thus, once we have a Riemann surface $\CC/\Lambda$ from uniformizing a characteristic zero lift, after possibly changing coordinates\footnote{A lattice has many choices of basis: to apply Pinkall's construction we need a basis $\{2\pi, \tfrac{A}{2}+i\frac{L}{2}\}$ where $A,L$ satisfy the isoperimetric inequality on $\SS^2$.} we need only find a curve $C$ on $\SS^2$ with prescribed area and length.
Generically there is an infinite dimensional space curves with given $A,L$ so one has considerable freedom to impose various aesthetic constraints when embedding $\CC/\Lambda$ in $\RR^3$. 

% For concreteness, the family below is a pleasing collection of curves which contains a representative for every moduli for some choice of $a,b,n,\varphi$ (requiring some thought and numerics to track down)
% $$c_{a,b,n,\varphi}(t)=(\theta(t),\phi(t))=\left(t+a\sin(2nt), \varphi+b\cos(nt)\right)$$

\begin{figure}[h!tbp]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{img/HopfTori.jpg}
	\caption{Curves on $\SS^2$ and their corresponding tori, colored to match their usage in the paper.}
	\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}

% To construct an explicit isometric embedding $\CC/2\pi\Lambda\to \mathbb{S}^3$
% from such a curve $c(t)$ of the appropriate length and area, we slightly generalize \cite{banchoff}.  Let  $T(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)=(e^{i\alpha}\sin\gamma, e^{i\beta}\cos\gamma)$ be coordinates on the 3-sphere in $\CC^2$, and note that choosing a particular Hopf fibration, we may parameterize the fiber over $(\theta,\phi)\in\mathbb{S}^2$ as  $H_{(\theta,\phi)}(s)=T(\theta+s,s,\phi/2)$. This provides an explicit parameterization of $c(t)$'s torus-shaped Hopf preimage: $s+it\mapsto  H_{c(t)}(s)$.  We modify $H_{c(t)}(s)$ into an isometry in two steps.  First, we force the $s$ and $t$ curves to be orthogonal by sliding along the Hopf fibers by some factor $f$: a computation shows $F(s+it)= H_{c(t)}(s-f(t))$ works if $f^\prime(t)=\sin\left(\phi(t)/2\right)\theta^\prime(t)$.  Next we force the $s,t$ directions to be unit speed.  This is already true for $s$, but computations show the infintesimal arc along the $t$ curves is precisely half the infinitesimal arclength of $c(t)$ on $\mathbb{S}^2$. Thus $s+it\mapsto F(s,L^{-1}(2t))$ is the desired isometry, where $L$ is the arclength function of $c$.  We compute this map given $s+it\in\CC$ by

% \begin{itemize}
% \item Find $v$ such that $L(v)=\int_0^v \sqrt{\theta^\prime(x)^2\sin^2\phi(x)+\phi^\prime(x)^2}\,dx=2t$ (numerically)
% \item Given this $v$, compute $\theta=\theta(v)$, $\phi=\phi(v)$ and $f=\int_0^v \sin\left(\frac{\phi(x)}{2}\right)\theta^\prime(x)\,dx$
% \item  Given $v,f,\theta,\phi$, compute  $H_{(\theta,\phi)}(s-f)=(e^{i (\theta+s-f)}\sin\tfrac{\phi}{2} ,e^{i(s-f)}\cos\tfrac{\phi}{2})$
% \end{itemize}

% Computing the pullback metric reveals the $s$ direction to be arclength parameterized whereas the $t$ direction is stretched by half the infinitesimal arclength of $c(t)$ on $\mathbb{S}^2$.  That is, pulling back the Hopf torus' metric along $F$ gives the same result as pulling the flat metric on back along $G(s,t)=(s,L(t)/2)$, for $L(t)=\int_0^t \sqrt{\theta^\prime(t)^2\sin^2\phi(t)+\phi^\prime(t)^2}\,dt$ the spherical arclength of $c$.  Together this implies $F\circ G^{-1}$ is a local isometry, which we can effectively compute given $u+iv\in\CC$:

% \begin{itemize}
% \item Find $t$ such that $L(t)=2v$ (numerically)
% \item Compute $f=\int_0^t \sin\left(\frac{\phi(x)}{2}\right)\theta^\prime(x)\,dx$
% \item  The image of $u+iv$ is then $H_{c(t)}(u+f)=(e^{i (\theta(t)+u-f)}\sin\tfrac{\phi(t)}{2} ,e^{i(u-f)}\cos\tfrac{\phi(t)}{2})$
% \end{itemize}


% Composing this with stereographic projection $(x,y,z,w)\mapsto (x,y,z)/(1-w)$ yields the desired realization of $\CC/2\pi\Lambda$ in $\RR^3$.

With an appropriate curve $C$ in hand, it only remains to write down an \emph{explicit} conformal map $\CC/\Lambda\to \RR^3$.  Parameterizing $C$ by $(\theta(t),\phi(t))$ and the hopf fiber above $(\theta,\phi)\in\SS^2$ as $H_{(\theta,\phi)}(s)=(e^{i(\theta+s)}\sin\tfrac{\phi}{2},e^{is}\cos\tfrac{\phi}{2})$ we can parameterize the torus $\eta^{-1}(C)$ by $s+it\mapsto H_{c(t)}(s)$ in $\SS^3$.  Starting from this (unfortunately non-conformal) map, we slightly generalize the techniques of \cite{banchoff} to adjust this map to an isometry, and then compose with stereographic projection.  To save others the trouble of computing this, we give the resulting map $\CC/\Lambda\to\RR^3$ below:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State {\bfseries Given} $s+it\in\CC$ with $t<L/2$ and a curve $(\theta(x),\phi(x))$ on $\SS^2$:
\State Numerically find $v$ such that $L(v)=\int_0^v (\theta^\prime(x)^2\sin^2\phi(x)+\phi^\prime(x)^2)^{1/2}\,dx=2t$
\State Compute $\theta=\theta(v)$, $\phi=\phi(v)$ and $f=\int_0^v \sin\left(\phi(x)/2\right)\theta^\prime(x)\,dx$
\State Compute $h=H_{(\theta,\phi)}(s-f)=(e^{i (\theta+s-f)}\sin\tfrac{\phi}{2} ,e^{i(s-f)}\cos\tfrac{\phi}{2})$
\State {\bfseries Return} stereographic projection $\sigma(h)$ for $\sigma\colon (x,y,z,w)\mapsto (x,y,z)/(1-w)$
\end{algorithmic}



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{Summary}

The pictures we sought seemed impossible to obtain initially, but in the end were realized through the combination of various constructions and theorems spanning several fields of math which don't always "talk to one another."
This work has reminded us once again to appreciate the unity of mathematics, and the utility of talking with friends in distant fields.  
We are excited to explore these ideas in more detail in a future paper.

