\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

This study demonstrates that offline RL trained on collected offline datasets can outperform traditional rule-based MRO methods in terms of reducing failures and other issues related to handovers. We have observed improvements up to $+7\%$ in previously unseen environments. 
%Most importantly these results are on test environments with different configuration than those where the train data were produced and collected; thus the method can generalize to unseen situations of load, and  velocity. 
Both DT and CQL methods showcase high performance, with DT performing slightly better for scenarios close to train data, and CQL on those further away. Using the same offline data our method can be trained with various types of rewards, % aiming for specific operation (e.g. limiting the CIO deviation from offset), 
thus offering higher flexibility compared to the rule-based MRO. This study has focused on tuning a unique source-target cell-pair for handovers. As a next step we plan to extend the RL study to account for the joint tuning and coordination of all cell-pairs in the network and differentiate tuning between UEs based on their service type.

