Summing all the equations gives $4z_1\in \Z$ and so the result follows.

Now we look at the different dilation profiles for the morphism $\Gamma\to K$. We start by looking at a dilation profile of type $\romanNumeralCaps{2}$, that without loss of generality we may assume to be $x_1 = x_2$. In that case, we have $x^{+-+-} = x^{-++-}$ and $x^{-+-+} = x^{+--+}$. In that case, we put $z_3 = \frac{1}{2}f'(x^{+-+-})$ and $z_4 = \frac{1}{2}f'(x^{+--+})$ and the system \eqref{systemKernel} stays as it is. This makes $z_3 = -z_4$ and so $\widetilde{f}'(x_1^+)=\widetilde{f}'(x_2^+)$. A similar argument works for type $\romanNumeralCaps{1}$.
