\begin{definition}
    We define $\psi:P_1\to P,~x\mapsto s(y)$ for any $x$ such that $2y=x$.
\end{definition}

 This map is well-defined: it does not depend on the choice of $y$ since if $2y=2y'$ we have $2y-2y'=0$ which means that $y-y'\in\operatorname{ker}(s)$, therefore we obtain $s(y)-s(y')=s(y-y')=0$. Moreover, there always exists such an element $y$ because as a group, $P$ is isomorphic to $(\R/\Z)^g$ for some $g\in\N$ which implies the surjectivity of the multiplication by 2 morphism.
