\begin{lemma}\label{isomorphism}
    The morphism $\psi$ satisfies $s=2\psi$ and $\psi$ is an isomorphism of groups.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	The formula follows from the definition of $\psi$: for $x = 2y$ we have
	\[ 2\psi(x) = 2s(y) = s(2y) = s(x). \]
    Injectivity can be seen as follows: $\operatorname{ker}(\psi)=\set{x\in P_1 \mid s(y)=0} = \set{x\in P_1\mid x=2y=0}$. Surjectivity of $\psi$ follows from the surjectivity of $s$, which in turn is clear from \cref{cor:4Id}.    
