%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Introduction
{\em Introduction.}~ 
Density functional theory (DFT) can predict properties  of materials from solely the knowledge of the atomic species in the materials and an approximation for the exchange-correlation (xc) energy; i.e., a functional of the particle density that maps the relevant interacting many-body problem onto an effective single-particle problem \cite{Jones2015}.
Given that the exact xc-energy functional of DFT is not known, empiricism can   be reduced only by knowing the exact properties of the xc-energy functional\cite{PerdewSchmidt2001}.

Generalizations of DFT hold the promise of yielding  solutions for materials that can be described via Schr\"odinger-Pauli equations, involving two-component spinors. In Spin-DFT\cite{BarthHedin:72},  the interacting problem is mapped onto an auxiliary non-interacting problem
reproducing the interacting particle and spin densities.
Thus, one must solve the  single-particle  equation  
\begin{align}\label{KSSDFT}
\left[-\frac{1}{2}
\nabla^2+v_s +
\mu_{B} \vec{B}_s \cdot \vec{\sigma}  \right]\Phi_k= \epsilon_k \Phi_k
\end{align}
where $\mu_{B} = \frac{1}{2}$ (in Hartree atomic units) and $\Phi_i$ are two-component Pauli spinors. The Kohn-Sham (KS) system reproduces the interacting particle density, via
$n = \sum_{k} f_{k} \Phi_k^{\dagger} \Phi_k$,
and each component of the interacting spin magnetization, via 
$m^a = \sum_{k} f_{k} \Phi_k^{\dagger} \sigma^a\Phi_k$,
 where the ${f_{k}}$ are occupation (often, integer) numbers.
The effective potentials are 
$v_s = v +v_{\rm H} + v_{\rm xc}$,
${B}^a_s = {B}^a +{B}^a_{\rm xc}$,
where  $v_{\rm H}$ is the usual Hartree potential.
The ${\rm xc}$-potentials are functional derivatives of the 
${\rm xc}$-energy functional, $E_{\rm xc}[n,\vm]$, with respect to the corresponding conjugate densities. Namely,
$v_{\rm xc} = \frac{\delta E_{\rm xc}[n,\vm]}{\delta n}$,
and
$
B^a_{\rm xc} =  \frac{\delta E_{\rm xc}[n,\vm]}{\delta m^a}
$.
Thus, $E_{\rm xc}[n,\vm]$ is the key quantity that needs to be approximated. Although Eq. \eqref{KSSDFT} reproduces the interacting spin {\em density}, it does not reproduce necessarily the (paramagnetic) spin {\em current} $\vec{J}_{\mu}$. 
The latter quantity enters the exact equation of motion
\begin{equation}\label{eq:moft}
\frac{\partial \vec{{m}}}{\partial t}+ \partial_\mu \vec{{{J}}}_{\mu} =
\vec{B} \times \vec{{m}} 
\end{equation}
which plays a central role in theories of magnetization dynamics and spintronics \cite{bhandary2023dynamical,dewhurst2020element,sharma2022making,geneaux2024spin,sanvito2007spintronics,sanvito2011molecular,sutton2015manipulating}. In this context, it was noticed long ago\cite{Capelle2001} that Eq. \eqref{eq:moft} implies that the xc magnetic field must  have a component orthogonal to the magnetization: this creates an ``xc spin torque'' which accounts for the difference between the exact spin current and the KS spin current
\begin{align}\label{eqn:wt}
        \vm  \times \vB_{\rm xc}  = \partial_\mu \left[  \vec{J}_{\mu} - \vec{J}_{s,\mu} \right ]
\end{align}
where $\vec{J}_{s,\mu} =  \sum_{k}  \frac{f_{k}}{2 i} \left\{ \left[ \partial_{\mu} \Phi_k^{\dagger} \right] \vec{\sigma} \Phi_k -   \Phi_k^{\dagger} \vec{\sigma} \left[ \partial_{\mu} \Phi_k  \right]\right\}$ is the (paramagnetic) spin current of the KS system.

Since then, the quest to find xc fields that generate an accurate xc spin torque has dominated the scene of SDFT for non-collinear magnetism\cite{Sharma2007a,ScalmaniFrisch:12,Bulik2013,eich2013transverse,eich2013trans2,Ullrich2018,Dewhurst2018,petrone2018efficient,goings2018current,komorovsky2019four,desmarais2021spin2,Hill2023,Pu2023,tancogne2023,Hill2023,Moore2025}.
While Eq. \eqref{eqn:wt} is a promising starting point, the challenge lies in  that the difference between the exact spin current and the KS spin current  is an uncontrolled quantity, which is beyond the reach of SDFT, and therefore cannot be used to derive local constraints  that would reduce the empiricism in the construction of approximate $\vB_{\rm xc}$. Furthermore,
systems including extra magnetic interactions (such as vector potentials and spin-orbit couplings) are beyond self-consistent 
SDFT treatments.

The only constraint that could be derived from  Eq. \eqref{eqn:wt} is the zero-torque theorem
\begin{align}\label{eqn:zttsdft}
 \int d^3r \,   \vm  \times \vB_{\rm xc} = \vec{0}\;
\end{align}
following from the fact that the electron-electron interaction does not exert a net torque on the magnetization. However, this constraint is too weak to provide useful guidance for improving approximations. 
We show below that both Eq. \eqref{eqn:wt} as well as Eq. \eqref{eqn:zttsdft}  have to be further extended to
arrive at a solution for a wide domain of applicability.


Spin-current density functional theory~\cite{VignaleRasolt:87,VignaleRasolt:88,Bencheikh:03} (SCDFT)  provides a resolution, in principle, to the above difficulties. In this theory the spin current (as well as the regular current) is a legitimate basic variable, even when vector potentials and spin-orbit couplings are considered. The latter cannot be ignored in applications for spintronics.\cite{vasili2025molecular,picozzi2025spin,mellnik2014spin,fert2008nobel} A remarkable exact condition — the U(1)$\times$SU(2) gauge invariance of the xc-energy functional~\cite{VignaleRasolt:88,Bencheikh:03}  imposes, by construction, proper xc-torques. 

In practice, however, the development of SCDFT has long been hindered by the lack of suitable density functional approximations which could be applied to atomistic systems with reasonable success.   This has changed dramatically in recent years with the advent of U(1)$\times$SU(2) gauge-invariant xc functional of the Meta-GGA (MGGA) class, which use the kinetic energy density and its spin-dependent generalization as building blocks~\cite{Pittalis2017,DesmaraisSCMGGA,DesmaraisNCmSCAN}. Because these building blocks are naturally expressed in terms of single-particle orbitals, they are most efficiently handled in the framework of the generalized Kohn-Sham (GKS) theory~\cite{Seidl1996,DesmaraisGKS}.

The GKS theory  is a sharpening of the traditional KS theory in which the minimization of the energy with respect to densities is replaced by a minimization with respect to single-particle orbitals, in terms of which the densities are expressed. This framework has been shown to be at least equivalent to, and possibly more general than the traditional KS framework under broad assumptions of noninteracting $v$-representability~\cite{Seidl1996,DesmaraisGKS,Nrep}.

In this work, we show that SpinCurrent-DFT within the GKS-MGGA framework provides a resolution of the xc-spin torque problem not only in principle, but {\em also} in practice. In addition, physically sound non-collinear equilibrium conditions as well as adiabatic dynamics can be obtained also when vector potentials and spin-orbit couplings are included. 


{\em Spin-Current-DFT -- the basics}.~
 The KS equations in SpinCurrent-DFT
have the form of single-particle Pauli equations~\cite{VignaleRasolt:88,Bencheikh:03}
\begin{equation}\label{KSeq}
\left[ \frac{1}{2}\left( -i   \nabla + \frac{1}{c} { \mathbfcal{A} }_\KS  \right)^2 +  {\cal V}_\KS  \right] \Phi_k = \varepsilon_k \Phi_k \; ,
\end{equation}
where~\cite{noteGDFTnotation}  $\Phi_k$ are two-component spinors,
$
{ \mathcal{A} }_{\KS,\mu} =\left( A_\mu +  A_{{\rm xc},\mu} \right) +  \vec{\sigma} \cdot \left(  \vec{A}_\mu + \vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu} \right)
$ is the effective spin-dependent vector potential,
$
{\cal V}_\KS = \left( v + v_{\rm H}+ v_{\rm xc} \right) + \vec{\sigma} \cdot \left( \vec{B} + \vec{B}_{\rm xc} \right) 
+ \frac{1}{2c^2} \left[ \left(  {\bf A} + \sigma^a {\bf A}^a \right)^2-  {\mathbfcal A}^2_\KS \right]
$ is the effective spin-dependent scalar potential,
 $\vec{\sigma}$ denotes the vector of Pauli matrices. The effective potentials are derived from the xc energy functional $E_{xc}[n,\vec m,j_\mu,\vec J_\mu]$ according to the formulas
${\frac{1}{c}}A_{{\rm xc},\mu}= \delta E_{\rm xc}/\delta j_\mu$
and
$
{\frac{1}{c}}\vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu} = \delta E_{\rm xc}/\delta \vec{J}_\mu
$.
The current and the spin-current densities are given by
$
j_\mu = \frac{1}{2i} \sum_{k}^{\rm occ} {f_k} \left\{ \Phi^\dagger_k \left[ \partial_\mu \Phi_k\right] -  \left[ \partial_\mu \Phi^\dagger_k \right] \Phi_k \right\}$
and ${\vec{J}}_\mu = \frac{1}{2i} \sum_{k}^{\rm occ} {f_k} \left\{ \Phi^\dagger_k \vec{\sigma}  \left[ \partial_\mu \Phi_k \right] -  \left[ \partial_\mu \Phi^\dagger_k \right] \vec{\sigma} \Phi_k \right\}
$ respectively. Also notice that we redefined the external fields as $\mu_B {B}^{a} \to {B}^{a}$ and $\frac{\mu_B}{2}  {\bf A}^a \to {\bf A}^a$
to simplify  notation.

Next, let us  consider  the following gauge transformations.
A local U(1) transformation, is defined by
$  \Phi \rightarrow    \exp\left[ \frac{i}{c}  \chi \right] \Phi $
where $   \chi $ is a scalar function of the position.
A local SU(2) transformations is  defined by
$  \Phi \rightarrow \exp\left[ \frac{i}{c} {\vec{\lambda} \cdot \vec{\sigma} } \right]  \Phi $
where $\vec{\lambda}$ is a  vector function of the position. The overall effect of the above transformations is  to rotate the spinors and each of the phases of their components as well.  These position-dependent  transformations implement a continuum of {\em local} rotations. The two ``rotations'' are performed conjointly in U(1)$\times$SU(2) transformations.

With the exception of the particle density $n$, which is invariant under U(1)$\times$SU(2), all the other variables of SCDFT are transformed according to precise rules (See SM, Sec. I, Eqs. S3 and S6).
The spin density is rotated in spin space, the current density is ``translated" by a gauge-dependent term, and the spin current density is both rotated and translated. It is a fundamental result of SCDFT\cite{VignaleRasolt:88,Bencheikh:03} that the xc energy functional is invariant under these transformations, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{EXCInvariance}
E_{xc}[n,\vec m',j_\mu',\vec J_\mu']=E_{xc}[n,\vec m,j_\mu,\vec J_\mu]\,,
\end{equation} 
where the primed variables are connected to the unprimed variables by the U(1)$\times$SU(2) transformation. We emphasize that this crucial result follows from the U(1)$\times$SU(2) symmetry of the Coulomb interaction and holds even when the system under consideration includes external potentials that break the U(1)$\times$SU(2) symmetry of the full Hamiltonian. No comparable symmetry exists for the xc functional of SDFT.


While the importance of enforcing U(1) invariance in the xc-energy functional approximations has long been recognized,   delivering major improvements\cite{Dobson93,Becke-j02,TP05,Pittalis2006,Pittalis07,Rasanen09,bates2012harnessing,furness2015current,Raimbault2015,Berger2015,Raimbault2016,tellgren2018uniform,laestadius2019kohn,pemberton2022revealing,richter2023meta},
the conjoint U(1)$\times$SU(2) invariance has been considered only occasionally. We argue below that progress in capturing proper xc torques and noncollinear solutions has correspondingly lagged behind.

From the equation of motion of the particle density and magnetization,
both for the interacting system and KS system, in view of the fact that all  the fundamental relevant densities are
reproduced, one readily derives~\cite{VignaleRasolt:88,Bencheikh:03}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}\label{c1_2}
\partial_\mu   \left[ n  {A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}   + {\vm \cdot  \vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}  } \right] = 0\;,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}\label{c2_2}
\vec{J}_\mu  \times \frac{1}{c}  \vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}  + \vec{m} \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc}  + \frac{1}{2c} \partial_\mu \left[ \vec{m} {A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}  + n \vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu} \right]   =  \vec{0}\;.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
In particular, Eq. \eqref{c2_2} 
shows that the torque of $\vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}$ on $ \vec{J}_\mu$ and the torque of $\vec{B}_{\rm xc} $ on $\vec{m}$  are balanced by the divergence of an ``xc diamagnetic spin current", about which more will be said below. 

Focusing on ground states, it has been proved that  
Eq. \eqref{c1_2} and Eq. \eqref{c2_2} are satisfied by any xc-energy functional that is
U(1)$\times$SU(2) gauge invariant, i.e., satisfies Eq.~(\ref{EXCInvariance})~\cite{VignaleRasolt:88,Pittalis2017}. Therefore,  gauge invariance is a key exact condition to be satisfied for any xc functional approximation to be admissible.

For example, the  local spin density approximation (LSDA) can be applied straightforwardly to non-collinear states by aligning the $z$-axis with the local direction defined by $\vm$ \cite{KueblerWilliams:88,NordstroemSingh:96,OdaCar:98}. 
One can readily verify that this approximation  is U(1)$\times$SU(2) invariant and satisfies the xc-torque equations
of SpinCurrent-DFT, although with vanishing  xc-torque. 
Therefore, the LSDA is an
admissible starting point for SCDFT. It is however a  very crude approximation, as
it does {\em not} account for the  longitudinal and transverse spin gradients and does not include the functional dependence of $E_{xc}$ on spin currents.


In order to capture the  dependence of the xc-energy functional on the {\it relative orientation} of neighboring spins, several attempts have been made
to include \emph{transverse} gradients of the  magnetization\cite{landau2013electrodynamics}
at the level of the generalized-gradient approximations (GGAs)\cite{ScalmaniFrisch:12,Bulik2013,Dewhurst2018,Moore2025}. Unfortunately, these attempts break U(1)$\times$SU(2) invariance and, thus, are not admissible in SCDFT. More recent attempts in the framework of MGGA also break U(1)$\times$SU(2)  invariance at several levels\cite{noteGB}.

The difficulty of  implementing the constraint of Eq. \eqref{EXCInvariance} in practical density functional approximation has held back the development of SCDFT  for  several decades. Next we show how the discovery of U(1)$\times$SU(2)-invariant MGGA variables  breaks the impasse and unlocks the power of SCDFT within the framework of the generalized Kohn-Sham formalism.

{\em Spin-Current-DFT --- unleashed}.~ 
Meta-GGAs (MGGAs) are yielding major improvements in modern DFT~\cite{SCAN,CHEMSCAN,LAK,SKALA}.
They represent the xc energy functional as the spatial integral of an xc energy density of the form ${\epsilon}_{\rm xc}(n,\nabla n, \tau$) 
where $\epsilon_{xc}$ is a regular function of three  arguments and $\tau = 1/2\sum_i^{\rm{occ}}|\nabla \varphi_i|^2$ is the kinetic energy density. MGGAs can also depend on the Laplacian of the particle density, yielding to no substantial difference in the arguments that follow.

An analysis based on the short-range behavior of the exchange hole showed that  MGGAs can be turned into a U(1)$\times$SU(2)-invariant approximation satisfying Eq.~(\ref{EXCInvariance}) 
via the minimal substitution~\cite{Pittalis2017}
 $\tau \rightarrow   \tilde{\tau}  = \left(  \tau - \frac{j_\mu j_\mu }{ 2n }  \right)  + \left( \frac{\vec{m}  \cdot \vec{\tau}}{n}   - \frac{ \vec{J}_\mu \cdot \vec{J}_\mu  }{ 2n  }  \right)  +   \frac{ \partial_\mu \vec{m}  \cdot  \partial_\mu \vec{m} }{8 n }$.
We stress that the kinetic energy density
$\tau = 1/2\sum_k^{\rm{occ}}  \Big(  \partial_\mu \Phi^{\dagger}_k \Big) \Big( \partial_\mu \Phi_k \Big)$ and  the spin-kinetic energy density
$
{\vec{\tau}} = 1/2 \sum_{k}^{\rm occ} \Big(  \partial_\mu \Phi^{\dagger}_k \Big) \vec{\sigma} \Big( \partial_\mu \Phi_k \Big)$
are   built from two-component spinors, and transverse  gradients of the spin density are included in $( {\partial_\mu} \vec{m}  )  \cdot ( {\partial_\mu} \vec{m})$. The $\tilde{\tau}$ is the cornerstone of  the recent U(1)$\times$SU(2) gauge-invariant extension of the electron localization function to non-collinear states \cite{zwoelf}.  It also enters the definition of the gauge invariant extension of the SCAN fuctional (the non-collinear-mSCAN, or briefly mSCAN)\cite{DesmaraisNCmSCAN}, which fixes serious failures  of the SCAN functional
for magnetic materials.
\cite{noteGDFTmin}

MGGAs find their most comfortable home in DFT via the generalized KS (GKS) formalism\cite{Seidl1996,perdew2017understanding}. The minimization of the energy functional is carried out with respect to the spin-orbitals, with the  densities regarded as functions of the spin-orbitals. This is at least as general as the standard Kohn-Sham procedure within the space of densities that are non-interacting $v$-representable\cite{Seidl1996,DesmaraisGKS}.

For the MGGA considered in this  work~\cite{noteGDFTsquare} the variational principle leads to the GKS equations in the form\cite{DesmaraisGKS} .

\begin{widetext}
\begin{align}\label{GKS-SCDFT}
\left\{ \frac{1}{2} \left[ -i \nabla +  \frac{1}{c} \mathbf{A}  \right]^2  - \frac{i}{2c} \left( \partial_\mu \vec{A}_\mu \cdot \vec{\sigma} + \vec{A}_\mu \cdot \vec{\sigma} \partial_\mu \right)+ \vec{B} \cdot \vec{\sigma} + v + v_{\rm H} + \hat{V}^{\rm SL}_{\rm xc}\right\} \Phi_k = \epsilon_k \Phi_k
\end{align}
\end{widetext}
where $\hat{V}^{\rm SL}_{\rm xc} \equiv
\mathcal{V}_{\rm xc}  - \frac{1}{2}\partial_\mu  {\cal M }_{{\rm xc},\mu}  \partial_\mu 
- \frac{i}{2c} \left[ \partial_\mu \mathcal{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}  + \mathcal{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu} \partial_\mu \right]$ and $\mathcal{V}_{\rm xc}=v_{\rm xc} + \vec{\sigma} \cdot \vec{B}_{\rm xc}$, ${\cal M }_{\rm xc}=\mu_{\rm xc} + \vec{\sigma} \cdot \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}$  and $\mathcal{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}=A_{{\rm xc},\mu} + \vec{\sigma} \cdot \vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}$.  
From Eq. \eqref{GKS-SCDFT}, it is apparent that the GKS equation replaces the several {\em local} potentials of  the regular KS-SCDFT [see Eq. \eqref{KSeq}]  by
a {\em single} semi-local (SL) operator $\hat{V}^{\rm SL}_{\rm xc} $. Yet, $\hat{V}^{\rm SL}_{\rm xc}$ is obtained in terms 
of several {\em local} potentials: $v_{\rm xc}$, ${\cal M }_{\rm xc}$, $\vec{B}_{\rm xc}$, $A_{{\rm xc},\mu}$, $\vec{A}_{{\rm xc},\mu}$. In detail, for a MGGA as the nc-mSCAN --- with energy density per unit volume $\epsilon_{\rm xc} = {\epsilon}_{\rm xc}(n,\partial_\mu n, m, \tilde{\tau}(n, \vec{m},\partial_\mu \vec{m},j_\mu,\vec{J}_\mu,\tau,\vec{\tau}))$ --- one gets 
the explicit expressions are available in Sec. II of the SM, Eqs. (S24)-(S28).
Also notice that
${\cal M }_{\rm xc}$ can be understood as an effective (inverse) mass accounting for both an Abelian (i.e. particle), $\mu_{\rm xc}$, and  a non-Abelian  (i.e., spin), $\vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}$, component (more below).

Eqs.~\eqref{c1_2} and \eqref{c2_2} must also be generalized. Crucially, from the U(1) invariance of the functional we get
[see Sec. III in SM] 
\begin{align}\label{eq:U1}
\partial_\mu \left[ \frac{1}{c} A_{xc,\mu}  n +  \frac{1}{c} \vec{A}_{xc,\mu} \cdot \vec{m} +  \mu_{\rm xc}  j_\mu 
+ \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \cdot \vec{J}_\mu \right] =0\;,
\end{align}
which generalizes Eq.~\eqref{c1_2}, and from the SU(2) invariance [see Sec. IV in SM] 
\begin{widetext}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:SU2}
\frac{1}{c}  \vec{A}_{xc,\mu} \times \vec{J}_\mu  + \vec{B}_{xc} \times \vec{m}  +  \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \times \vec{\tau} - \frac{1}{4} \partial_\mu \left[ \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \times \partial_\mu \vec{m} \right] -\frac{1}{2} \partial_\mu  \left[ \frac{1}{c}  \vec{A}_{xc,\mu} n  + \mu_{\rm xc}  \vec{J}_\mu  + \frac{1}{c} A_{xc,\mu} \vec{m} + \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} j_\mu \right] = \vec{0}\;,
\end{eqnarray}
\end{widetext}
which generalizes Eq.~\eqref{c2_2}.


These expressions can be greatly simplified by taking into account the specific form of our functional, i.e., the fact that the squared currents enter in combination with the kinetic energy densities in a single variable $\tilde \tau$. Making use of this fact we quickly discover
\begin{align}\label{eq:U1-2}
\frac{1}{c} A_{xc,\mu}  n +   \mu_{\rm xc}  j_\mu  =0\;,~~   \frac{1}{c} \vec{A}_{xc,\mu} \cdot \vec{m}  + \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \cdot \vec{J}_\mu   =0\;,
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}\label{eq:SU2-2}
 \frac{1}{c}  \vec{A}_{xc,\mu}n  + \mu_{\rm xc}  \vec{J}_\mu  = \vec{0}\;,~~  \frac{1}{c} A_{xc,\mu}  \vec{m} + \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} j_\mu  = \vec{0}\;.
\end{align}

Eq.~(\ref{eq:U1-2}) shows that the mass renormalization of the (paramagnetic) current, arising from terms $\mu_{\rm xc}  {j}_\mu$ and  $\vec \mu_{\rm xc} \cdot \vec{J}_\mu$,  cancels the spurious xc diamagnetic currents $\frac{1}{c}  {A}_{xc,\mu} n$ and $\frac{1}{c}  \vec {A}_{xc,\mu} \cdot\vec m$. As a result, Eq.~(\ref{eq:U1}) is identically satisfied.
Eq.~(\ref{eq:SU2-2}) similarly states the cancellation between the mass renormalization of the spin current and the spurious xc diamagnetic contributions to this quantity.


Furthermore, it is evident from the considered MGGA form
\begin{align}\label{eq:MGGAttau}
\vec{A}_{xc,\mu} \times \vec{J}_\mu  = \vec{0}\,.
\end{align}
This shows that the non-Abelian xc-vector potential does not impart a torque on the spin currents. Only the physical spin-orbit coupling can  exert a {\em direct} torque on the spin current.


Eq. \eqref{eq:U1} together with Eqs. \eqref{eq:SU2}, \eqref{eq:U1-2}, \eqref{eq:SU2-2}, and \eqref{eq:MGGAttau} are the first set of key results of this work. Together they  
yield the set of key conditions
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:newteqs}
\begin{align}\label{eqn:newteqs-1}
    \vm \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc} +\vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}  =   \frac{1}{4} \partial_\mu \left[ \left( \partial_\mu \vec{m} \right) \times  \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}  \right]\;. 
\end{align}
Integration, readily, implies
\begin{align}\label{eqn:newteqs-2}
\int d^3r ~ %\Bigg\{ 
 \left[ \vm \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc} +\vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \right]
= \vec{0}\;.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
The latter  relation can also be derived by  generalization of the zero-torque theorem [see
Sec. V in SM].

Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-1} and Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-2} upgrade  Eq. \eqref{eqn:wt} and Eq. \eqref{eqn:zttsdft} to a larger domain of applicability. Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-1} and Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-2} include an additional xc-torque (i.e., $\vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} $)
and the  right hand side of Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-1}, that is, $1/4 \partial_\mu \left[ \left( \partial_\mu \vec{m} \right) \times  \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}  \right]$,
suggestively resembles the divergence of the  {\em phenomenological} ``spin currents'' of the {Landau-Lifshitz equation}\cite{maekawa2017spin}. 
To the best of our knowledge, these terms have been missed or gone unnoticed in  previous MGGA-related works on non-collinear magnetism. Below we provide numerical estimates in relevant systems.



Eqs.~\eqref{eq:U1-2}-\eqref{eq:MGGAttau} also  imply that the total (i.e. paramagnetic plus diamagnetic) GKS  particle- and spin-currents are --- in form --- identical to their exact interacting counterparts.
As a result, we conclude that the {\em adiabatic} application~\cite{noteGDFTadiabatic} 
of the proposed MGGAs in time, yields
 particle density and spin magnetization that follow --- in form --- the same time-dependent equations as in the (exact) interacting case; i.e., changing back to Hartree atomic units (and, thus, not absorbing $\mu_{\rm B}$ in the definitions of the fields with spin index as done above)
\begin{widetext}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \tilde{n}  }{\partial t}+\partial_\mu 
\left[
\tilde{j}_{\mu}+\frac{1}{c} \tilde{n} A_\mu +\frac{1}{4c} \vec{\tilde{m}}\cdot \vec{A}_\mu
\right] =0  \label{22}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \vec{\tilde{m}}}{\partial t}+\partial_\mu \left[ \vec{{\tilde{J}}}_{\mu}+ 
\frac{1}{c}
\vec{\tilde{m}} A_\mu + \frac{1}{4c} \tilde{n}  \vec{A}_\mu\right] =
\vec{B} \times \vec{\tilde{m}} +  \frac{1}{2c}  \vec{A}_\mu \times
\vec{\tilde{J}}_\mu  \label{35}
\end{equation}
\end{subequations}
\end{widetext}
where $\tilde{n}$,  $\tilde{m}^a$, $\tilde{j}_{\mu}$, 
${\tilde{J}}^a_{\mu}$ are all computed by evaluating the considered U(1)$\times$SU(2) gauge invariant MGGAs on the instantaneous  spinors [see Sec. VI in SM]. 
Eq. \eqref{22} and Eq. \eqref{35} are the final set of key equations of this work. 
They provide a substantial extension of previous works on the continuity equation for the particle density and the generalized continuity equation for the spin density in the context of GKS.\cite{bates2012harnessing,Baer18,richter2023meta}


