{\em Applications:}~ We provide a numerical demonstration of the relevance of the GKS torques Eqs. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-1} and \eqref{eqn:newteqs-2} in both non-collinear and non-coplanar magnets. 

We performed self-consistent SCDFT calculations with the MGGA (the non-collinear mSCAN functional),\cite{DesmaraisNCmSCAN} including spin-orbit coupling, on the unsupported Cr (111) monolayer in the N{\'e}el state [details are provided in Sec. VII in SM]. We employed the lattice parameter of the Ag (111) surface, which leads to geometrical frustration of the monolayer and, correspondingly, a two-dimensional non-collinear spin wave. The same system was also previously studied in relation to the xc torque in SDFT.\cite{SharmaGross:07,bulik2013noncollinear} Results are reported in the contour plots of Fig. 1, which provides (from left to right), the spin magnetization $\vec{m}$, as well as the components of the xc torques $\vec{m} \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc}$ and $\vec{\tau} \times \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}$ normal to the Cr(111) plane. The plot of $\vec{m} \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc}$ (middle panel) shows the expected atomic-shell-like structure that was also reported in previous exact-exchange optimized effective potential calculations.\cite{SharmaGross:07}
Importantly, our GKS calculations also provide the spin-kinetic $\vec{\tau} \times \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}$ xc torque (rightmost panel) which  is on the same order of magnitude as $\vec{m} \times \vec{B}_{\rm xc}$ (see color scales at bottom of figure).  As for the zero-torque theorem (ZTT) in GKS provided in Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-2}, we obtained $\int d^3r \  \vec{m} \times   \vec{B}_{\rm xc}= \vec{0}$, as well as $\int d^3r \  \vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}= \vec{0}$ in the case of the Cr (111) monolayer. 

To  discuss an example with non-zero integrated quantity, $\int d^3r \  \vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}$, we also performed analogous calculations on a noncoplanar magnet which was built from the Cr$_4$ cluster in a tetrahedral arrangement (full geometry is provided in Sec. VII of the SM). This provided an integrated $\Vert \int d^3r \  \vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc} \Vert$ of 6.82 $\times$10$^{-4}$ a.u., while the GKS ZTT $\int d^3r \  [\vec{m} \times   \vec{B}_{\rm xc}+\vec{\tau} \times   \vec{\mu}_{\rm xc}]= \vec{0}$ was satisfied, numerically, down to 1$\times$10$^{-27}$ a.u.



{\em Conclusions}.~
Difficulties in capturing exchange-correlation  spin torques non empirically, have  persisted in Spin-DFT (SDFT). 
Fundamentally, spin-torques involving spin-currents, vector potentials, and spin-orbit couplings are beyond the reach of SDFT.

Here we have presented a solution based  on first principles, which  substantially extends the early formulation of SpinCurrent-DFT and its range of applicability.

The newly derived relations, Eqs. \eqref{eq:U1}-\eqref{eq:MGGAttau}  are  equilibrium properties of non-collinear states, which follow from a gauge invariant extension of meta-generalized-gradient approximations.

Furthermore,  Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-1} and Eq. \eqref{eqn:newteqs-2} upgrade Eq. \eqref{eqn:wt} and Eq. \eqref{eqn:zttsdft} to a larger domain of applicability.
We evaluated the key terms 
for an unsupported Cr monolayer and for a 
noncoplanar  Cr$_4$ magnet.

Remarkably, this approach  allows us to calculate key elements of the adiabatic spin dynamics [see Eq.s \eqref{22} and \eqref{35}], with wide applicability to the study of spin fluctuations and excitations of magnetic systems.





