Finally, we blend the extended function $\tilde{f}$ to zero on either end by multiplying by a windowing function which is smooth and symmetric on both sides. The smooth window, as shown in Figure~\ref{window_fn}, is determined by the smooth step-up in the transition region $[-\delta,0]$ or $[1,1+\delta]$. Let $\psi\in C^\infty([0,1])$ as shown in Figure~\ref{psi}, with $0\le\psi\le 1$, and
\begin{align*}
    \psi(x)&=0, \text{ for $x$ in a neighborhood of $[0,\alpha]$}\\
    \psi(x)&=1, \text{ for $x$ in a neighborhood of $[1-\beta,1]$}
\end{align*}
Then, such $\psi$ generates a smooth step-up function on $[0,1]$ defined by
\[
    S(x) = \psi(\alpha(1-x)+(1-\beta)x).
\]
The window function is then defined by
\[
W(x;\delta) = \begin{cases}
    0,&x\in (-\infty,-\delta)\cup (1+\delta,\infty),\\
    1,& x\in [0,1],\\
    S(1+\frac{x}{\delta}),& x\in (-\delta,0),\\
    S(1-\frac{x-1}{\delta}), &x\in (1,1+\delta).
\end{cases}
\]
We need $M$ values of $\psi$ sampled in the transition region $(\alpha,1-\beta)$ in order to apply $W(x;\delta)$ to $\tilde{f}$. These samples are 
\begin{eqnarray}
    \psi(\alpha+\eta j)\hspace{10pt}j=1,2,3,\dots,M \hspace{10pt} \text{where} ~\eta = \frac{1-\alpha-\beta}{M-1},\label{transition_samples}
\end{eqnarray}
and are shown in Figure~\ref{M-samples}. After scaling and translation, the interval $(\alpha,1-\beta)$ corresponds to the interval $(-\delta,0)$ in Figure~\ref{window_fn} and the step size $\eta$ corresponds to the grid step size $h$. Therefore, the samples in \eqref{transition_samples} are the required samples of $W(x;\delta)$ in the transition region $(-\delta,0)$. By multiplying these samples with the columns of $E_l$ and $E_r$ element-wise, we have the following discrete windowed extension operator 
\[
    \tilde{f}_W = \begin{pmatrix}
        \widetilde{E}_lQ_l^T&0&0\\
        I&0&0\\
        0&I&0\\
        0&0&I\\
        0&0&\widetilde{E}_rQ_r^T
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        f_l\\f_c\\f_r
    \end{pmatrix},
\]
where $\widetilde{E}_l$ and $\widetilde{E}_r$ are the counterparts of $E_l$ and $E_r$ respectively, blended to zero by $\psi.$ A demonstration of the 1D blending to zero is shown in Figure~\ref{blendtozero}.
