\subsubsection{Parameter description and selection}\label{parameter_selction}
Here, we briefly describe the parameters used in the 2D-FC algorithm. Additionally, we select some specific values for these parameters to get the best result.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item $d$: number of near-boundary points used to blend $f$ to zero along each normal (subsection~\ref{blend-to-zero}).
    \item $C$: number of continuation points in the 1D blending to zero process (subsection~\ref{blend-to-zero}).
    \item $k_1$: normal step size (subsection~\ref{curvilinear_grid})
    \item $k_2$: boundary step size (subsection~\ref{curvilinear_grid})
    \item $h$: step size used to construct Cartesian grid $\mathcal{H}$ (subsection~\ref{curvilinear_grid})
    \item $n_r$: refinement factor used to obtain the curvilinear grids along each normal vector (Remark~\ref{refine_factor})
    \item $N=N_x\times N_y$: number of points in the uniform Cartesian grid (subsection~\ref{curvilinear_grid})
    \item $B$: number of boundary discretization points (subsection~\ref{curvilinear_grid})
    \item $M-1$: the degree of the interpolating polynomial (subsections~\ref{FC_values} and \ref{FC_on_cartpts})
\end{itemize}
For a given step size $h$ used to construct the Cartesian grid $\mathcal{H}$, we choose normal step size $k_1$ and boundary step size $k_2$ to coincide with $h$. The parameters $C=25$ and $n_r=6$ were used in the 1D blending to zero procedure described in Subsection~\ref{blend-to-zero}. Finally, we set the interpolating polynomial degree $(M-1)=d$ for all matching point numbers $d$ considered except the interpolation-degree experiments presented in Example~\ref{performance}.

