\subsection{Reinforcement learning setup}
\label{sec:rl_setup}
% \paragraph{RL algorithm} 
We train the LN algorithm with Algorithm \ref{alg:actor_critic}. As explained in Sections \ref{subsec:observation_space}--\ref{subsec:action_space}, the parameter $K$ controls how many order book levels the algorithm observes and how deep it may post orders into the order book. 
In our simulations, the price rarely moves more than five ticks throughout the execution horizon. Therefore, we choose $K=6$ for the dimension of the simplex $\SI_K.$ This means that the RL algorithm may place limit orders up to five ticks above the best bid price 
and observes the volumes on the first five levels on both sides of the book. To encode the policy $\pi_\theta$ and the value function $V_\vartheta,$ we choose two separate feed-forward neural networks with two hidden layers, $\tanh$-activation functions in the hidden layers and no activation functions in the last layers. The output layer of the policy network has $K$ nodes, and the last layer of the value function network has one node. We start by setting the covariance matrix $\Sigma$ to the identity matrix and then progressively reduce its values according to the linear schedule \eqref{covariance_scaling}. We choose an initial bias $b=(-1,-1,\dots,-1)\in\R^{K}$ for the policy network. We obtain from \eqref{mean} that
\begin{equation*}
	\label{bias}
	\E \left[ \log\left( \frac{a^K}{a^k} \right)  \right] = 1 
	\quad 
	\text{and}
	\quad 
	\E \left[ \log\left( \frac{a^k}{a^K} \right)  \right] = -1, 
	\quad 
	k = 0,1,\dots,K-1, 
\end{equation*}
showing that action $a_K$ is more likely than actions $a_0, \dots, a_{K-1}.$ This means the algorithm does not place any orders at the start of the training cycle, which ensures that the algorithm observes complete trajectories up to the terminal time $T$ early in the training process, preventing it from getting stuck in local minima. Further implementation details concerning the training of the LN algorithm are discussed in Appendix \ref{sec:hyper_parameters}.

\subsection{Logistic-normal vs Dirichlet distribution}
First of all, we observe that in all three market simulations, the LN algorithm outperforms the DR algorithm, which is visible in Table \ref{table:results} and Figure \ref{fig:densities_for_all_markets}. Figure \ref{fig:return_convergence} shows the development of the episode returns over the full training cycle. The upper panel shows the results for 20 lots, and the lower panel shows the results for 60 lots. We observe that in all experiments, except when the agent executes 20 lots in the market only containing noise traders, the rewards for the LN algorithm converge faster than for the DR algorithm. This suggests that the LN algorithm is a superior alternative to the DR algorithm in this allocation problem. 
As the performance of the LN and DR algorithms is qualitatively similar, we focus our attention on the performance of the LN algorithm relative the the heuristic benchmarks in the following sections. 

