From the subplots in Fig.~\ref{Figure_3D}, it can be observed that as the packet size increases, the probabilistic delay bound also increases. This is because larger packets require longer transmission times within the network, which raises the probabilistic delay bound. Additionally, at lower total end-to-end delay $T$, the probabilistic delay bound is higher, since a stricter delay requirement increases the probability of exceeding that threshold.
