    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{panel_01.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{(a)} Chladni states for selected fractional indices $i/N$, using $N=2^{14}$. From left to right: $i=(2,~2^8,~2^{12},~2\cdot2^{12},~3\cdot2^{12})$. \textbf{(b)} Energy of Chladni states (Ising Hamiltonian evaluated on binarized eigenvector) versus normalized index, for several system sizes (see legend, $N=L^2$). The curve collapse across sizes, with an inflection point at half the system size, separating the positive from the negative energy region. The red dashed line indicates zero energy. \textbf{(c)} Monte Carlo evolution of selected Chladni states (see legend) at $T=0$. States with negative energy (below the red dashed line) remain stable, while those with positive energy decay.}
    \label{fig:panel_1}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:panel_1}(a) shows different selected Chladni states as described by their corresponding eigenvectors for a 2D square lattice (further states for multiple lattices are reported in SM~\cite{SM}). \FigRef{fig:panel_1}(b) shows the energy corresponding to each normalized Chladni state as a function of the normalized growing eigenvectors. The energy curve exhibits a characteristic S-shape independent of the system size, but strongly dependent on the lattice geometry (see SM \cite{SM}). Note how the emergent geometrical patterns exhibit, for the first $N/2$ eigenvectors, an associated energy of the Ising Hamiltonian lower than or equal to zero. This is because these states correspond to large-scale vibrational modes (i.e., related to small eigenvalues), presenting large domain walls that remain stable except for single interactions that have zero energy gap. 

To monitor their stability, we simulate the kinetically constrained Ising dynamics \footnote{That is, making no flip when $\Delta E=0$. The updating rule when $\Delta E=0$ is key for this point. Hence, the Ising-Glauber dynamics smooth domain walls, making some patterns unstable.} (see \cite{Godreche2005} for further details). We have performed extensive \gls{mc} simulations, preparing the system at $T=0$ on selected Chladni states and following its temporal evolution, as reported in \FigRef{fig:panel_1}(c). In particular, we observe that states exhibiting negative energy (representing low eigenmodes or large spatial scales) are temporally stable, i.e., the system remains stuck on them. Note that any thermal fluctuation makes the system evolve to the ferromagnetic state (i.e., the uniform eigenvector).

The same phenomenology arises in different two-dimensional triangular, hexagonal, and two-scale lattices (the latter inspired by real materials \cite{Falsi2024}, see SM \cite{SM}), each generating distinct Chladni states.  Notably, as a consequence of the discrete nature of the lattice, arbitrary superpositions of low-energy Chladni modes --much like in quantum systems-- are expected to generate stable Ising configurations at zero temperature (see SM \cite{SM}). This motivates us to use the Laplacian eigenvectors, ${\ket{\lambda_j}}$, as a natural basis for decomposing any frozen spin configuration $\ket{m_i} = \sum_j \braket{\lambda_j | m_i} \ket{\lambda_j}$. This expansion is well-defined because the set of Laplacian eigenvectors, ${\ket{\lambda_i}}$, forms a complete basis in the functional space $L_2(\mathcal{D})$ of measurable and square-integrable functions on $\mathcal{D}$ \cite{Sudhir2017}. We refer to this as \emph{\acrfull{tmd}}, providing a genuine spectral representation of any spin pattern in terms of the underlying network geometry.

%, which constitute an orthonormal set when the eigenfunctions are conventionally normalized to unit $L_2$ norm 

%Note that this fact naturally explains the ultrametric structure and degeneracy of states of \gls{sg} systems as discussed below.

\emph{\textbf{TMD in frustrated antiferromagnetic systems}.} We apply TMD to the paradigmatic case of a triangular antiferromagnet, where lattice geometry prevents the system from achieving global long-range order \cite{Drisko2017, MagicMoments}. The Laplacian eigenbasis remains unchanged in this case, as a global sign change in the interactions flips the sign of the full energy spectrum. Fig. \ref{fig:panel_2}(a) confirms that the energy curve for Chladni states mirrors the ferromagnetic case under $E\rightarrow-E$ symmetry, and low-index modes correspond to metastable configurations at $T=0$ (see SM~\cite{SM}). This reflects the absence of anti-ferro/ferromagnetic symmetry, as opposed to the square lattice. However, when applying an infinitesimal temperature $T=\epsilon$, the absence of the ordered phase allows the system to surf into the intrinsic degeneracy \cite{Shokef2011}, now identified by multiple combinations of lower eigenvectors. Initial conditions with negative energy retain a memory footprint in the long-time state, reflecting proximity to local metastable basins (see SM~\cite{SM}). Remarkably, any final state of the system can always be reconstructed using \gls{tmd}. \FigRef{fig:panel_2}(b) shows the TMD of a relaxed antiferromagnetic metastable state initiated at random at $T=0$, and the corresponding reconstructed pattern.
