Figure~\ref{fig:panel_2}(c) shows the reconstructability index, $R$ (i.e., the overlap with the simulated configuration, site by site) for a triangular antiferromagnetic lattice initialized at selected Chladni states at $T=0$. Fig.~\ref{fig:panel_2}(d) reports the reconstructability of a randomly initialized configuration after long-time relaxation for different values of $T$ (a critical point is expected to appear at $T\sim0.5$ as reported in \cite{Yamada1995, Kawamura1984,Chung2006} for diverse dynamical lattice models). A clear change appears at the expected critical point: in the paramagnetic phase, the higher $T$, the higher the number of modes needed to reconstruct the spin pattern.  The common threshold across different spin models strengthens our geometrical prediction. In any case, we demonstrate that any state is fully reconstructable.


