\emph{\textbf{TMD in Spin Glasses}.} We turn now to \glspl{sg} \cite{Edwards1975}, the paradigm of non-ergodic behavior, where frustration from competing ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic bonds \cite{Bray1987, Castellani2005} leads to multiple metastable states that trap the system in local minima, preventing a full exploration of the configuration space \cite{Mezard1984}. We consider the random-bond Ising model, where a fraction $p$ of links change to have a negative sign. To incorporate frustration, we adopt the signed Laplacian, $\slapl=|D|-J$, which accounts for both attractive and repulsive couplings, and has been shown to capture the topological features of frustrated interactions \cite{Iannelli2025}. Now, $D=\sum_j|J_{ij}|$ represents the (absolute) weighted degree of each site. \FigRef{ChladSG}(a) shows the reconstructability index for the random-bond Ising model on a 3D cubic lattice at $T=0$, initialized in different Chladni states. The sharp jump at the initialization state supports the previous hypothesis that these correspond to pure states of the \gls{sg} dynamics \cite{Iannelli2025}. As before, the reconstructability index exhibits a shift at the paramagnetic phase (see \FigRef{ChladSG}(b)),  confirming its ability to detect the loss of mesoscopic order in generic short-range frustrated models.

\textbf{\emph{Chladni states as topological autoencoders.}}
A direct application of \gls{tmd} is the decomposition of binary or weighted patterns into superpositions of eigenmodes, enabling spectral reconstructions based on the system's topology. As a proof of concept, we apply this approach to the MNIST dataset, considering each image as a spin configuration. Fig.\ref{ChladSG}(c) shows the average \gls{mse} between original and reconstructed digits as a function of the number of eigenvectors used, across different network topologies. In particular, the optimal topology for reconstruction notably depends on the nature of the input pattern (see SM~\cite{SM}). Also, low-index eigenmodes --associated with large-scale topological features-- already yield accurate reconstructions, acting as efficient "information carriers," much like the compression layer of an autoencoder. This motivates a reinterpretation of \gls{tmd} as a topological autoencoder, where the topological eigenbasis plays the dual role of encoder and decoder. Unlike standard autoencoders, the encoding here is topologically driven and analytical, requiring no training. For clarity, Fig.~\ref{ChladSG}(d) shows the resulting projection of the first three MNIST digits using the corresponding transformation matrix (see SM~\cite{SM}). Noisy digits can then be classified by identifying the nearest surface associated with each digit class.

In short, the information is encoded directly on the network or lattice, as emerges from the Ising Hamiltonian, where the Laplacian eigenvectors define the topological modes of the system. This is intrinsically different from Laplacian eigenmaps~\cite{Belkin2003}, which generate a data-driven graph from the input pattern. Here, metastable modes naturally act as a topological autoencoder, where the pure states of the system dictate encoding and decoding. The challenge now is to identify such modes in real materials and compare them with those predicted here. In a nutshell, we provide a compact and interpretable spectral representation of frozen spin or dipole textures, actually relevant to frustrated systems and materials with microstructural disorder~\cite{Xin2024, Simonov2020, Ramesh2019, Nahas2020}.

