\emph{\textbf{Outlook}.} Our results reveal a unifying topological framework for describing metastability in discrete spin systems. By identifying Laplacian eigenmodes as the fundamental resonances governing frozen configurations, we establish a direct correspondence between energy landscapes and the spectral geometry of the interaction network. For classical Ising ferromagnets, the picture of Chladni states is evocative of stable states on an ice rink: the system remains locally trapped until being destabilized by thermal noise. Our perspective reinterprets pure metastable states as topological standing waves whose binarized forms --Chladni states-- serve as effective attractors in the non-equilibrium dynamics of ferromagnets, antiferromagnets, and \glspl{sg}.

A key implication is the possibility of band-pass filtering metastable configurations via their spectral decomposition. This may enable the identification of dominant spatial modes, introducing a new tool for quantifying memory and structure in glassy and frustrated states. This spectral filtering opens the route to the topological diagnosis of metastability and correlated disorder.

Crucially, \gls{tmd} enables the decomposition and reconstruction of arbitrary spin configurations using the topological eigenbasis. This positions \gls{tmd} as a topological analogue of an autoencoder, where memory and pattern recognition intrinsically emerge from geometry rather than learned parameters. As a result, frozen configurations in disordered systems are no longer arbitrary but become predictable projections onto a discrete vibrational spectrum. This framework also opens new directions: it provides a spectral diagnostic tool for detecting the scale and structure of memory in non-ergodic systems; it offers a basis for designing experimentally-driven topologically robust information encoders in real materials, where metastable magnetic states function as memory units; and it reveals a deeper connection between critical phenomena, spectral topology, and unsupervised learning, suggesting general principles of high-dimensional topological compression. In our view, this geometric encoding suggests a topological mechanism for memory formation in physical systems, bridging statistical mechanics and learning theory through spectral geometry.

